!> Adam on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface. Bias-corrected full-batch first and second
!> moments, denominator sqrt(vhat)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after applying its
!> update. No line search or general nonconvex convergence guarantee; O(p) reset local state. The
!> caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate
!> in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and
