!> LevenbergMarquardt on the ResidualFunction callback interface. Forms J transpose J plus scalar
!> damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE,
!> multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after
!> rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints.
!> O(np^2+p^3) work. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are
!> borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use
!> local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads
!> requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
!> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - K. Levenberg (1944), A Method for the Solution of Certain Non-Linear Problems in Least Squares,
!>   https://doi.org/10.1090/qam/10666; D. W. Marquardt (1963), An Algorithm for Least-Squares
!> Estimation of Nonlinear Parameters, https://doi.org/10.1137/0111030
!>
!> See also: `LevenbergMarquardtOptions`, `trace_optimize()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: LevenbergMarquardt
contains
    !> Run LevenbergMarquardt from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Forms J transpose
    !> J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half
    !> residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm
    !> even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints.
    !> O(np^2+p^3) work.
    procedure :: optimize => levenbergmarquardt_optimize
    !> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
    procedure :: options => levenbergmarquardt_options
end type

