!> NelderMead on the ArbitraryFunction callback interface. Local simplex reflection, expansion,
!> contraction and shrink search. Starts with per-coordinate displacements initial_simplex_scale and
!> stops when simplex diameter and value spread are small. Those conditions do not prove stationarity
!> or a global minimum. O(p^2) simplex storage. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
!> callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a
!> callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a
!> mutable objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a
!> uniform convergence certificate.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::optim::NelderMead optimizer;
!> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 2000
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - J. A. Nelder and R. Mead (1965), A Simplex Method for Function Minimization,
!>   https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/7.4.308
!>
!> See also: `NelderMeadOptions`, `trace_optimize()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: NelderMead
contains
    !> Run NelderMead from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Local simplex reflection,
    !> expansion, contraction and shrink search. Starts with per-coordinate displacements
    !> initial_simplex_scale and stops when simplex diameter and value spread are small. Those conditions
    !> do not prove stationarity or a global minimum. O(p^2) simplex storage.
    procedure :: optimize => neldermead_optimize
    !> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
    procedure :: options => neldermead_options
end type

