!> SGD on the DifferentiableSeparableFunction callback interface. Uses the mean gradient of each
!> mini-batch, reshuffling component indices each epoch when enabled. Full objective remains the sum of
!> components. Stops on absolute full-objective change per epoch. Nonempty components and positive
!> batch_size are required; seeds reproduce the same build, not a cross-library shuffle transcript. The
!> caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate
!> in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and
!> propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external
!> synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::optim::SGD optimizer;
!> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_epochs); // 100
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - H. Robbins and S. Monro (1951), A Stochastic Approximation Method,
!>   https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177729586. The fixed-rate reshuffling variant here differs from
!> their decreasing-step stochastic approximation.
!>
!> See also: `SGDOptions`, `trace_optimize()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: SGD
contains
    !> Run SGD from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses the mean gradient of each
    !> mini-batch, reshuffling component indices each epoch when enabled. Full objective remains the sum of
    !> components. Stops on absolute full-objective change per epoch. Nonempty components and positive
    !> batch_size are required; seeds reproduce the same build, not a cross-library shuffle transcript.
    procedure :: optimize => sgd_optimize
    !> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
    procedure :: options => sgd_options
end type

