!> implementation, not a general SQP QP solver: takes an objective-gradient step, then sequential
!> linearised row corrections using squared Jacobian-row norms plus regularization. Its stopping
!> condition compares constraint norm and the constant absolute step_size to tolerance, not the actual
!> displacement or Lagrangian gradient. The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks.
!> Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback
!> throws. Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
!> objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform
!> convergence certificate.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::optim::SQP optimizer;
!> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition,
!>   https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are
!> specified above.
!>
!> See also: `SQPOptions`, `trace_optimize()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: SQP
contains
    !> Run SQP from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Restricted projection-like
    !> implementation, not a general SQP QP solver: takes an objective-gradient step, then sequential
    !> linearised row corrections using squared Jacobian-row norms plus regularization. Its stopping
    !> condition compares constraint norm and the constant absolute step_size to tolerance, not the actual
    !> displacement or Lagrangian gradient.
    procedure :: optimize => sqp_optimize
    !> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
    procedure :: options => sqp_options
end type

!> Configuration for TrustRegionNewton; fields retain their documented native defaults. Supply finite
!> values in the supported ranges. Controls are copied at construction; validation, stopping and loss
!> scaling belong to optimize().
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> dm::optim::TrustRegionNewtonOptions o;
!> std::print("{}\n",o.max_iterations); // 200
!> ```
!>
!> See also: `TrustRegionNewton`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: TrustRegionNewtonOptions
contains
    !> Gets C++ field `initial_radius`: Positive starting Cauchy-step radius. Default 1.0.
    procedure :: get_initial_radius => trustregionnewtonoptions_get_initial_radius
    !> Sets C++ field `initial_radius`: Positive starting Cauchy-step radius. Default 1.0.
    procedure :: set_initial_radius => trustregionnewtonoptions_set_initial_radius
    !> Gets C++ field `max_radius`: Maximum radius after successful Cauchy steps. Default 1000.0.
    procedure :: get_max_radius => trustregionnewtonoptions_get_max_radius
    !> Sets C++ field `max_radius`: Maximum radius after successful Cauchy steps. Default 1000.0.
    procedure :: set_max_radius => trustregionnewtonoptions_set_max_radius
    !> Gets C++ field `max_iterations`: Maximum native iterations/epochs as described by optimize(); zero
    !> performs no updates. Default 200.
    procedure :: get_max_iterations => trustregionnewtonoptions_get_max_iterations
    !> Sets C++ field `max_iterations`: Maximum native iterations/epochs as described by optimize(); zero
    !> performs no updates. Default 200.
    procedure :: set_max_iterations => trustregionnewtonoptions_set_max_iterations
    !> Gets C++ field `tolerance`: Stopping threshold; its actual gradient/change/displacement/scale
    !> meaning is method-specific. Default 1e-8.
    procedure :: get_tolerance => trustregionnewtonoptions_get_tolerance
    !> Sets C++ field `tolerance`: Stopping threshold; its actual gradient/change/displacement/scale
    !> meaning is method-specific. Default 1e-8.
    procedure :: set_tolerance => trustregionnewtonoptions_set_tolerance
end type

!> TrustRegionNewton on the HessianFunction callback interface. Cauchy-point variant: clips a
!> steepest-descent ray using gHg and the current radius. Accepts any strict objective decrease and
!> doubles the radius, otherwise halves it. It does not compute a model-agreement ratio, dogleg step or
!> exact trust-region subproblem. Stops on gradient norm or tiny radius; O(p^2) Hessian work. The
!> caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed; coordinates mutate
!> in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws. Calls use local optimizer state and
!> propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable objective across threads requires external
!> synchronisation. Normal return alone is not a uniform convergence certificate.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::optim::TrustRegionNewton optimizer;
!> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition,
!>   https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are
!> specified above.
!>
!> See also: `TrustRegionNewtonOptions`, `trace_optimize()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: TrustRegionNewton
contains
    !> Run TrustRegionNewton from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Cauchy-point
    !> variant: clips a steepest-descent ray using gHg and the current radius. Accepts any strict objective
    !> decrease and doubles the radius, otherwise halves it. It does not compute a model-agreement ratio,
    !> dogleg step or exact trust-region subproblem. Stops on gradient norm or tiny radius; O(p^2) Hessian
    !> work.
    procedure :: optimize => trustregionnewton_optimize
    !> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
    procedure :: options => trustregionnewton_options
end type

