!> Exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters family): simple exponential smoothing (no trend, no
!> seasonality), Holt linear trend (with optional damping), and seasonal Holt-Winters (additive or
!> multiplicative), unified under one class the way R's HoltWinters() and statsmodels'
!> ExponentialSmoothing() are -- which components are active is controlled entirely by
!> ExponentialSmoothingOptions::trend/seasonal.
!>
!> Initial level/trend/seasonal-index states are set once from a simple classical
!> decomposition of the first one or two seasonal cycles and then held fixed; only
!> the smoothing parameters (alpha, beta, gamma, phi) are fit, by minimizing
!> one-step-ahead sum of squared errors with DifferentialEvolution.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: ExponentialSmoothing
contains
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::options`.
    procedure :: options => exponentialsmoothing_options
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::alpha`.
    procedure :: alpha => exponentialsmoothing_alpha
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::beta`.
    procedure :: beta => exponentialsmoothing_beta
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::gamma`.
    procedure :: gamma => exponentialsmoothing_gamma
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::phi`.
    procedure :: phi => exponentialsmoothing_phi
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::observations`.
    procedure :: observations => exponentialsmoothing_observations
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::sse`.
    procedure :: sse => exponentialsmoothing_sse
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::sigma2`.
    procedure :: sigma2 => exponentialsmoothing_sigma2
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::log_likelihood`.
    procedure :: log_likelihood => exponentialsmoothing_log_likelihood
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::aic`.
    procedure :: aic => exponentialsmoothing_aic
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::bic`.
    procedure :: bic => exponentialsmoothing_bic
    !> In-sample one-step-ahead fitted values, aligned to the input series. The warm-up prefix used to set
    !> initial states (see class docs) is copied from the observed values (residual exactly zero there).
    procedure :: fitted_values => exponentialsmoothing_fitted_values
    !> C++ `datamunge::stats::ExponentialSmoothing::residuals`.
    procedure :: residuals => exponentialsmoothing_residuals
    !> Point forecasts for the next `horizon` periods.
    procedure :: forecast => exponentialsmoothing_forecast
end type

!> B-spline design factory, analogous to R's splines::bs(). Owns a clamped basis on fixed boundaries,
!> reused for training and prediction. By default the first basis column is omitted, so a formula's
!> intercept does not duplicate the partition of unity. No fitting or smoothing is performed. Const
!> evaluations are independent and safe to run concurrently.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::Bspline basis(0, 1, {0, 0.5, 1});
!> const auto design = basis.eval({0, 0.5, 1});
!> std::print("{} {}\n", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - C. de Boor (1972). "On calculating with B-splines". Journal of Approximation
!>   Theory 6(1), 50-62. doi:10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9.
!>
!> See also: `bs()`, `fda::BSplineBasis`, `stats::LM`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: Bspline
end type

!> A least-squares B-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x). `dm::BSR` for short.
!>
!> The fit is ordinary least squares (no roughness penalty). Throws std::invalid_argument for
!> mismatched/empty inputs or too few basis functions for the degree, and std::runtime_error when
!> the design is rank deficient (some basis function has too little data under it; use fewer
!> basis functions / knots).
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: BSplineRegression
contains
    !> Evaluates the fitted curve at `x`. Points outside the fitted domain take the value at the nearest
    !> boundary, i.e. constant extrapolation.
    procedure :: predict => bsplineregression_predict
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::coefficients`.
    procedure :: coefficients => bsplineregression_coefficients
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::fitted`.
    procedure :: fitted => bsplineregression_fitted
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::residuals`.
    procedure :: residuals => bsplineregression_residuals
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::r_squared`.
    procedure :: r_squared => bsplineregression_r_squared
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::basis_size`.
    procedure :: basis_size => bsplineregression_basis_size
    !> C++ `datamunge::BSplineRegression::degree`.
    procedure :: degree => bsplineregression_degree
    !> The full knot sequence (boundary knots repeated degree + 1 times).
    procedure :: knots => bsplineregression_knots
    !> The [min, max] interval the basis covers.
    procedure :: domain => bsplineregression_domain
end type

!> A P-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x) (Eilers & Marx). `dm::PSR` for short.
!>
!> Minimizes ||y - Bβ||² + λ ||D_d β||², where D_d is the order-d difference matrix on the
!> coefficients (d = 2 by default). Uniform knots continue past both ends of the data
!> without repeated boundary knots. When d <= degree+1, polynomials of degree < d
!> go unpenalized: λ → ∞ gives the least-squares line for d = 2. A rich basis can
!> include functions with no data under them if the data identify the penalty nullspace.
!> Without an explicit λ, it is chosen
!> by minimizing generalized cross-validation, GCV(λ) = n·RSS / (n − edf)².
!>
!> The polynomial-nullspace claim requires uniform spacing; it need not hold for irregular
!> supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only when the design identifies
!> the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns its fit and permits concurrent
!> const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
!> this work. Very large lambda can make the equations numerically singular.
!>
!> **Example**
!>
!> ```
!> const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
!> std::print("{:.6f} {:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); // 0.428571 2.142857
!> ```
!>
!> **References**
!>
!> - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
!>   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
!>
!> See also: `fda::fit_pspline()`, `smoothing_path()`, `summary()`.
type, extends(rosetta_object), public :: PSplineRegression
contains
    !> Evaluates the fitted curve at `x`. Points outside the fitted domain take the value at the nearest
    !> boundary, i.e. constant extrapolation.
    procedure :: predict => psplineregression_predict
    !> Fitted coefficients in basis order.
    procedure :: coefficients => psplineregression_coefficients
    !> Predictions at the training coordinates.
    procedure :: fitted => psplineregression_fitted
    !> Observed minus fitted training responses.
    procedure :: residuals => psplineregression_residuals
    !> In-sample 1-RSS/TSS, with the constant-response convention of PSplineFitResult.
    procedure :: r_squared => psplineregression_r_squared
    !> The smoothing parameter λ used for the fit (the GCV minimizer when not given).
    procedure :: lambda => psplineregression_lambda
    !> Order of adjacent coefficient differences.
    procedure :: penalty_order => psplineregression_penalty_order
    !> Effective degrees of freedom, trace of the hat matrix.
    procedure :: effective_df => psplineregression_effective_df
    !> Generalized cross-validation score n·RSS / (n − edf)² of the fit.
    procedure :: gcv => psplineregression_gcv
    !> Number of basis functions, including boundary functions.
    procedure :: basis_size => psplineregression_basis_size
    !> Polynomial degree of each spline segment.
    procedure :: degree => psplineregression_degree
    !> The full knot sequence, including the `degree` extra knots past each end of the data.
    procedure :: knots => psplineregression_knots
    !> The [min, max] interval the basis covers.
    procedure :: domain => psplineregression_domain
    !> Fitted order-d coefficient differences, whose squared norm is roughness.
    procedure :: coefficient_differences => psplineregression_coefficient_differences
end type

